## Extending the course Bulletins scrape fr Multiple departments 

[ISA](https://bulletin.miamioh.edu/courses-instruction/isa/) and [MKT](https://bulletin.miamioh.edu/courses-instruction/mkt/). From the logic show in class

Note that if you are doing just a few departments

```{r bulletin}
dept_abbr = c('acc', 'bus', 'isa', 'mkt')
# line below illustrates how the url can be constructed by paste0 (i.e., now space) the base_url, dept_abbreviation and the forward slash

dept_abbr = c('acc', 'bus', 'isa', 'mkt')
courses_df = data.frame()  # initialize container

for (dept_name in dept_abbr) {
  print(dept_name)
  cat(rep("-", 5)) 
  dept_url = paste0(
  "https://bulletin.miamioh.edu/courses-instruction/", dept_name, "/")

# for the titles, I will use the selector gadet and for the descriptions, I will use the inspector (to show your alt. CSS selectors fro what we did on Mon)
webpage = rvest::read_html(dept_url)

webpage |> rvest::html_elements("strong") |> rvest::html_text2() -> titles
webpage |> rvest::html_elements("#sc_sccoursedescs > div > p.courseblockdesc") |> rvest::html_text2() -> descs

df = data.frame(title = titles, desc = descs)
df$title[1] |> print()

courses_df = rbind(courses_df, df)
cat(rep("-", 5))
}

```

## Re-examing the Prior exmaple with the 'Purrr' package

My preffered appraoch with the purrr function is to frst creat my own cuntuonn and it within the package

```{r}
scrape_mu_bulletin = function(dept_url){
  
webpage = rvest::read_html(dept_url)

webpage |> 
  rvest::html_elements("strong") |> 
  rvest::html_text2() -> titles
webpage |> 
  rvest::html_elements("#sc_sccoursedescs > div > p.courseblockdesc") |> 
  rvest::html_text2() -> descs

df = data.frame(title = titles, desc = descs)

return(df)
}

test = scrape_mu_bulletin("https://bulletin.miamioh.edu/courses-instruction/bus/")
dept_abbr = c('acc', 'bus', 'isa', 'mkt')
my_urls = paste0(
  "https://bulletin.miamioh.edu/courses-instruction/", # single chr string
  dept_abbr, # vector of four obs
  "/" # single chr string
)
# the map function take on more inputs
# in this example, we only habe 1 input for our function 
# so we used map_( instead of map2_() [@ is if you had two inputs], p_map (){three or more inputs} )
# .x = the vector your are iterating over
courses_df_from_purrr = purrr::map_df(.x = my_urls, .f = scrape_mu_bulletin)
head(courses_df_from_purrr)
```








